## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about multi-word bit manipulations and safe 64→128-bit extension without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit reversal via two 64-bit reversals and swapping halves
- Parallel-bits reversal (mask/shifts)
- Endianness-insensitive mapping of bit indices

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverse64(x) {
  x = (x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n | (x & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n;
  x = (x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n | (x & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n;
  x = (x >> 4n) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n;
  x = (x >> 8n) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n;
  x = (x >> 16n) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n;
  x = (x >> 32n) | (x << 32n);
  return x;
}
function reverse128(hi, lo) {
  const revLo = reverse64(lo);
  const revHi = reverse64(hi);
  return { hi: revLo, lo: revHi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to streaming 128-bit values?
- How to verify correctness with random tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-level data packing and word-level efficiency.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (dilating)
- Inline masks and shifts for 32-bit results
- Morton codes for spatial indexing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t part1by1(uint16_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x &= 0x0000FFFF;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t morton2D(uint16_t x, uint16_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 3D Morton codes (x,y,z) and 64-bit result? 
- How would you adapt for non-power-of-two bit widths or streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary extraction and 128-bit layouts; practical in serialization, bitboard ops, or crypto.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lane bit extraction
- Masking and shifts
- Boundary checks

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t extractWord128(uint128 x, unsigned pos){
  switch(pos){
    case 0: return (uint32_t)(x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    case 1: return (uint32_t)((x.lo >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    case 2: return (uint32_t)(x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    default: return (uint32_t)((x.hi >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to return 16-bit or 64-bit words?
- Can you refactor to be branchless while preserving correctness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests constant-time, multi-word popcount without branches. Bridges knowledge of SWAR patterns from 64-bit into 128-bit by handling halves separately. It also checks handling of non-power-of-two widths.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR/popcount on 64-bit
- 128-bit word handling (hi/lo)
- Branch-free, mask-shift arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t countSetBits64(uint64_t v){
  v = v - ((v>>1)&0x5555555555555555ULL);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2)&0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint32_t countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return countSetBits64(x.lo) + countSetBits64(x.hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to 256-bit values?
- How to verify edge cases (all-ones, zero) across both halves?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests endianness understanding and safe 128-bit lane manipulation with pure bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts
- Byte reversal with masks
- 128-bit struct arithmetic
- Endianness swapping

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline uint64_t reverseBytes64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  x = (x << 32) | (x >> 32);
  return x;
}

uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = reverseBytes64(x.lo);
  r.lo = reverseBytes64(x.hi);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a compile-time constant shift? 
- How to verify with unit tests across edge cases (all zeros, all ones, cross-boundary values)?

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates capability to manipulate multi-word integers with pure bit tricks, a common requirement in crypto and low-level systems programming. It tests cross-lane thinking and branch-free implementation.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lane bit propagation between hi and lo.
- Branch-free 128-bit rotation using only shifts and OR.

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft1281(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
  r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to rotate left by k (0 <= k < 128) without branches?
- How would you test boundary cases like k=0, k=127, across hi/lo boundaries?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit arithmetic, two's-complement negation, and branchless masking, all crucial for low-level optimizations in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo limbs
- Two's-complement negation across limb boundaries
- Branchless bit masking using bitwise operations
- Edge cases: x==0 and bit in hi vs lo limb

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 extractLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = (~x.lo) + 1;
  uint64_t hiNeg = (~x.hi) + (loNeg == 0);
  uint128 mask;
  mask.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  mask.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  return mask;
}
```

## Examples
- Input: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}` → Output: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}`
- Input: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}` → Output: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}`

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a branchless popcount for 128-bit data using only bitwise ops, a core skill in performance-critical code paths like fingerprinting or anomaly detection.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless bit counting (SWAR)
- Apply per-64-bit halves independently
- Combine results without overflow or branches

## Code Example

```javascript
// Conceptual: 64-bit helper (C-like pseudocode)
unsigned int popcount64(unsigned long long x){
  x -= (x>>1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x>>2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (unsigned int)((x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56);
}
unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x){
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if you needed to accumulate counts in a streaming fashion?
- How does this interact with endianness in cross-language bindings?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of fast, branchless bit tricks and constant-time bit-scanning utilities common in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- Isolating least-significant 1: v = n & -n
- De Bruijn-based indexing: map a 5-bit index to a trailing-zero count
- Handling edge case n == 0 (return 32)
- Static lookup table of 32 entries

## Code Example
```c
uint32_t countTrailingZeros32(uint32_t n){
  static const int debruijn[32] = {0,1,28,2,29,14,24,3,30,22,20,15,25,17,4,8,31,27,13,23,21,19,16,7,26,12,18,9,6,11,5,10};
  if(n == 0) return 32;
  uint32_t v = n & -n;
  return debruijn[(v * 0x077CB531) >> 27];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit inputs?
- What are alternatives that avoid a static table, e.g., using binary search on bit groups?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses proficiency in low-level bit manipulation using only shifts and masks—skills essential for systems programming, embedded development, and cryptographic code. It demonstrates understanding of parallel bit-twiddling (SWAR/SIMD Within A Register) techniques.

## Key Concepts
- **Bit masking and shifting:** Extracting and repositioning specific bit ranges
- **Parallel processing:** Operating on multiple data elements (bytes) simultaneously via mask patterns
- **Byte-local vs. global reversal:** This approach reverses bits within each byte independently, unlike global bit-reversal algorithms
- **Magic constants:** The hexadecimal masks (`0xF0F0F0F0`, `0xCCCCCCCC`, `0xAAAAAAAA`) are standard patterns for these swaps

## Complete Code Example
```c
uint32 reverseBitsInEachByte32(uint32 x) {
  // Step 1: Swap adjacent nibbles (0xF0 = 11110000 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xF0F0F0F0) >> 4) | ((x & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);

  // Step 2: Swap adjacent 2-bit pairs (0xCC = 11001100 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xCCCCCCCC) >> 2) | ((x & 0x33333333) << 2);

  // Step 3: Swap adjacent individual bits (0xAA = 10101010 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xAAAAAAAA) >> 1) | ((x & 0x55555555) << 1);

  return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to implement a branchless, constant-time parity on 128-bit data, a common requirement in cryptography and error-detection code. It tests knowledge of SWAR reductions and handling of multi-word integers.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR parity reduction
- 128-bit representation via two 64-bit halves
- Constant-time bit-twiddling without branches
- Correctness over both halves

## Code Example
```javascript
unsigned parity128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t hi = x.hi, lo = x.lo;
  hi ^= hi >> 32; hi ^= hi >> 16; hi ^= hi >> 8; hi ^= hi >> 4; hi ^= hi >> 2; hi ^= hi >> 1;
  lo ^= lo >> 32; lo ^= lo >> 16; lo ^= lo >> 8; lo ^= lo >> 4; lo ^= lo >> 2; lo ^= lo >> 1;
  return (unsigned)((hi ^ lo) & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt parity128 to count parity modulo 4?
- What are performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit architectures?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply bit-hacking techniques across a 128-bit value by splitting into two 64-bit halves and summing, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts

- 64-bit popcount via parallel bit counting masks
- Treat 128-bit as two 64-bit blocks
- No loops or branches; purely bitwise ops

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return x & 0x7f;
}
unsigned int countBits128(uint128 x){
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you leverage __builtin_popcountll if available?
- How would you generalize to 256-bit types?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates the ability to implement a classic bit-twiddling technique (popcount) on 128-bit integers without branching. This skill is useful in hash tables, connectivity algorithms, and low-latency code.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting (divide-and-conquer approach)
- Splitting a 128-bit value into two 64-bit chunks
- Branchless arithmetic using bitwise masks

## Code Example
```c
unsigned popcount64(uint64_t v) {
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
    return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```
